Now Stage 2: policy.js and budget.js, plus extract.js (context). Let me write them.

First, `src/policy.js`:

```js
export const EDITORIAL_VERSION = 'editorial-v3';
export const CONTEXT_POLICY = 'full-text-v1';
export const VALIDATOR_VERSION = 'clef-guard-v1';

export const DEFAULT_REWRITE_MODEL = '@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fp8-fast';
export const DEFAULT_VALIDATOR_MODEL = '@cf/cloudflare/clef-flash';

export const MODEL_SPECS = {
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fp8-fast': { contextTokens: 128000, inNeuronsPerToken: 0.004091, outNeuronsPerToken: 0.034909, chat: 'messages', thinking: false },
  '@cf/qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-fp8': { contextTokens: 32768, inNeuronsPerToken: 0.004636, outNeuronsPerToken: 0.030455, chat: 'messages', thinking: true },
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.2-1b-instruct': { contextTokens: 128000, inNeuronsPerToken: 0.027273? ... }
};
```

Hmm, I don't know exact prices for llama 3.2 1b/3b. Let me not overstate. I'll define specs with conservative values, and mark estimates.

Actually simpler: `MODEL_SPECS` only for context windows, and neuron rates table. Let me define:

```js
export const CHEAP_MODELS = new Set([
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fp8-fast',
  '@cf/qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-fp8',
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.2-1b-instruct',
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct',
]);
export const MODEL_CONTEXT_TOKENS = {
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fp8-fast': 128000,
  '@cf/qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-fp8': 32768,
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.2-1b-instruct': 128000,
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct': 128000,
};
// Neurons per output/input token, derived from published per-million-token prices and
// Cloudflare's $0.011 per 1,000 neurons. Estimates used only for budget reservation.
export const MODEL_NEURON_RATES = {
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct-fp8-fast': { in: 0.00409, out: 0.03491 },
  '@cf/qwen/qwen3-30b-a3b-fp8': { in: 0.00464, out: 0.03045 },
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.2-1b-instruct': { in: 0.00291, out: 0.02018 },
  '@cf/meta/llama-3.2-3b-instruct': { in: 0.00782, out: 0.03300 },
  '@cf/cloudflare/clef-flash': { in: 0.00400, out: 0.03300 },
};
```

Hmm, llama-3.2-1b price? Unknown. I'll label as conservative placeholders. Actually I could avoid fake precision: use a single conservative fallback rate. But better to have per-model estimate; label clearly as estimates. To avoid presenting fabricated numbers, use a generic `ESTIMATED_NEURON_RATE = { in: 0.005, out: 0.035 }` fallback and known rates for llama-8b and qwen. Fine.

`policyFingerprint(env)`:
```js
export function policyFingerprint(env = {}) {
  const model = env.REWRITE_MODEL || DEFAULT_REWRITE_MODEL;
  const validator = env.VALIDATOR_MODEL || DEFAULT_VALIDATOR_MODEL;
  return `${model}|${EDITORIAL_VERSION}|${CONTEXT_POLICY}|${VALIDATOR_VERSION}|${validator}`;
}
```

`retireStaleRewrites(articles, fingerprint)`:
```js
export function retireStaleRewrites(articles, fingerprint) {
  let changed = false;
  const next = articles.map((article) => {
    if (article.rewriteStatus !== 'rewritten' && !article.rewriteFingerprint) return article;
    if (article.rewriteStatus === 'rewritten' && article.rewriteFingerprint === fingerprint) return article;
    changed = true;
    const { rewriteFingerprint, verification, ...rest } = article;
    return { ...rest, headline: article.originalHeadline, rewriteStatus: 'original' };
  });
  return { articles: next, changed };
}
```
Wait: an article could be `rewriteStatus: 'rewritten'` but missing fingerprint (old data) → retire. And article with fingerprint but status not rewritten? unlikely. Good.

Note: original objects should drop `verification` metadata. Also `headline` might equal original anyway.

Now `src/budget.js`:
```js
import { MODEL_NEURON_RATES } from './policy.js';
export const PRICE_PER_1000_NEURONS = 0.011;
export const DEFAULT_DAILY_NEURON_BUDGET = 5000;
export const ESTIMATED_TOKEN_CHARS = 2; // characters per estimated token (conservative for Hebrew+JSON)

export function estimateTokens(text) { return Math.ceil((String(text || '').length) / ESTIMATED_TOKEN_CHARS) + 8; }
